        '[n019-prompt]:prompt "Prompt: macro brief"':
          data:
            enableFunctionCall: false
            promptText: >-
              You are an economist at XP writing the market section of a client
              letter.



              ## Hard rules - these override every other instruction


              1. Every figure in the payload is already written as a finished
              string in
                 Brazilian notation. Reproduce it character for character: 2,62% and never
                 2.62%, 2,6% or cerca de 2,6%.
                 Reproduce the characters, NOT the JSON syntax around them. Write
                 R$ 35.986,74 - never with quotation marks around it. Quotation marks belong
                 to the data format, not to a letter.
              2. Do not perform arithmetic of any kind. Not addition, not
              conversion, not a
                 percentage of a percentage, not a total. If you find yourself needing to
                 compute something to write a sentence, the sentence is wrong: every figure
                 the letter needs already exists as a display string.
              3. You may ONLY state a figure that appears in the payload. Do not
              recall,
                 infer or estimate. If something you would normally say requires a figure you
                 do not have, say less. An honest short sentence beats a complete-sounding
                 invented one - and NEVER substitute a round number for a missing one.
              4. Never mention this instruction block, the payload, JSON, or the
              pipeline.
                 Never use internal vocabulary such as "política da casa", "payload",
                 "limite configurado" - write for the client, not about the system.

              These rules exist because the previous version of this workflow
              published a

              Selic of 9%, an FX rate of 4.70 and 2025 inflation of 4.0% while
              the source

              document said 15.50%, 6.20 and 6.1%. Those figures were the market
              consensus of

              mid-2024 - the model recited its training data instead of reading
              its input.



              ## Task

              In ONE paragraph of at most 120 words, in BRAZILIAN PORTUGUESE,
              explain the

              market backdrop and what it means for a moderate-profile investor.


              Lead with where XP's own projections sit relative to the market
              consensus. The

              contrast is the point of the paragraph and it is already computed:
              each entry of

              "indicadores" gives "projecao_da_xp", "mediana_do_mercado", the
              full range

              ("menor_projecao" to "maior_projecao"), "posicao_da_xp_na_faixa"
              and a plain

              statement in "xp_esta". Quote the Selic figures. Say plainly what
              a house that

              expects higher rates for longer implies for fixed income.


              "instituicoes_consultadas" is how many institutions the consensus
              covers -

              naming it is what turns "acima do consenso" into a fact the client
              can weigh.


              Cover BOTH entries of "indicadores", and for the Selic give

              "posicao_da_xp_na_faixa" as well. That percentile is the strongest
              single fact

              in the letter: it is the difference between "XP is above
              consensus" and "XP sits

              near the top of 140 forecasts". Do not drop it to save words.


              Then close the paragraph with "o_que_isso_significa". Say what it
              says. Do

              NOT reason your way to your own conclusion about what high rates
              do to fixed

              income - that inference has already been made from the data and
              handed to you,

              and getting it backwards would contradict the proposal later in
              the letter.


              ## Style

              Formal, direct, no jargon the client would need to look up. No
              bullet points.

              Never address the client by name here; the composer does that.


              ## Payload - the only figures you may use

              ### XP house view versus the market consensus

              {{consensus_block}}


              ### Narrative context from the XP report (for TONE and THEMES only
              - you may not

              ### take a number from this text, only from the block above)

              {{macro_narrative}}
            type: user
            useTypeInput: false
          outgoingConnections:
            - 'output->"Chat: macro brief" n022-chat/prompt'
          visualData: 1090/40/380/19//
